CloseCode — fix these five things
You are the developer of my phone app CloseCode. You have everything you need to build it. This file is the complete instruction for the next round of work — there is nothing else you need from me.

Build tested: 80 (CloseCode 0.80.1611, versionCode 80) Device: one Android phone, arm64, Android 16 Test project: com.rapas.redit — AGP 8.12.0, Kotlin 1.9.24, Compose BOM 2024.09.03, Media3 + MediaPipe + Vosk, minSdk 26, targetSdk 35

How to use this file
Everything is in here, including the test script.

Read "What is already working" so you do not break it.
Run the test script at the bottom of this file. It prints PASS/FAIL for every item below. That is our shared definition of done — when it prints FAIL 0, we are finished and nobody needs to write another report.
Work the items in order. Item 1 breaks the app for the user. Item 2 is the only one they can actually get stuck on.
Re-run the script after each item.
On build 80 the board reads: PASS 16, FAIL 6, SKIP 3.

The script is in a code block at the end. Save it next to the project as closecode-acceptance.sh and run:

sh closecode-acceptance.sh
It takes about eight minutes because it does real Gradle builds. It is safe: the only thing it changes is a temporary syntax error in app/build.gradle.kts, which it puts back immediately.

What is already working — do not break it
All of this was measured on the phone, on build 80.

Behaviour	Evidence
Settings installs the whole toolchain	JDK 17, Gradle 8.13, platform 35, build-tools 35.0.1, platform-tools, 4 SDK licenses, NDK 27.1.12297006, CMake 3.22.1 — each with a size and a Remove button
Component sizes match the disk	Native tools reports 1.8 GB; du says NDK 1.7 GB + CMake 73 MB
closecode build	BUILD SUCCESSFUL, exit 0, 74 s, APK 77,145,483 bytes
./gradlew assembleDebug	BUILD SUCCESSFUL, exit 0, 5m 49s
closecode check	engine:"gradle", errors:0, exit 0
The ./gradlew shim fixes AAPT2	it writes android.aapt2FromMavenOverride into $GRADLE_USER_HOME/gradle.properties before starting Gradle, so the arm64 aapt2 is used. This is a good solution — it reaches every wrapper invocation. Keep it
closecode setup --java21	installs OpenJDK 21.0.13 to …/file/j/lib/jvm/java-21-openjdk, exit 0
A failed build	exits 1 with one root cause and a log path: "summary":"Gradle failed (exit 1): file:///…/app/build.gradle.kts:69:29: Expecting an element"
local.properties	generated with sdk.dir
The install error message	clear signature_mismatch with both SHA-256 values and the remedy
Consent	request only asks; nothing is granted automatically
The five items
1. One signing key, on every path
This is the one that breaks the app. Please do it first.

The problem
The same project, built two ways, gets two different signatures:

Command	Certificate	SHA-256
closecode build	CN=Closecode Debug	30fae35d64645323304340c4f570a58377de8153477a5e334dc62c036a87f979
./gradlew assembleDebug	C=US, O=Android, CN=Android Debug	a6811008ffe66d61dd679b8a1954418a642e9a065de06cccd10a9bac3f41ddcc
The evidence
$ closecode build
$ apksigner verify --print-certs app/build/outputs/apk/debug/app-debug.apk
Signer #1 certificate DN: CN=Closecode Debug
Signer #1 certificate SHA-256 digest: 30fae35d64645323304340c4f570a58377de8153477a5e334dc62c036a87f979

$ ./gradlew assembleDebug
$ apksigner verify --print-certs app/build/outputs/apk/debug/app-debug.apk
Signer #1 certificate DN: C=US, O=Android, CN=Android Debug
Signer #1 certificate SHA-256 digest: a6811008ffe66d61dd679b8a1954418a642e9a065de06cccd10a9bac3f41ddcc
./gradlew signingReport shows where the second key comes from:

Variant: debug
Store: /data/user/0/com.closecode/files/opencode/home/config/.android/debug.keystore
SHA-256: A6:81:10:08:FF:E6:6D:61:DD:67:9B:8A:19:54:41:8A:64:2E:9A:06:5D:E0:6C:CC:D1:0A:9B:AC:3F:41:DD:CC
AGP takes the debug keystore from the Gradle daemon's Android location. On this phone that resolves to the OpenCode agent's own home, which ships the stock AOSP debug key. Your vault key CN=Closecode Debug is never used by that path.

There are three different debug keystores on the phone right now:

Used by	File	SHA-256
closecode build	the vault	30fae35d…
./gradlew	opencode/home/config/.android/debug.keystore	a6811008…
nothing today	file/gh/.android/debug.keystore	21cc1cc8…
For reference, the Gradle daemon sees:

user.home        = /data/data/com.closecode/file/gh
$HOME            = /data/user/0/com.closecode/files/opencode/home
ANDROID_USER_HOME = (unset)
Why it matters
The APK from the Gradle menu cannot update the app that closecode install put on the phone. Android rejects it with signature_mismatch, so the user installs one build, builds another, and is told to uninstall — which loses their data. This is the single worst thing in this list.

What to do
The debug keystore AGP finds must be your key — CN=Closecode Debug, 30fae35d… — in every location it can be found: at minimum the one the Gradle daemon resolves, and file/gh/.android/debug.keystore.
closecode build and ./gradlew must resolve the same user.home / ANDROID_SDK_HOME, so both paths sign the same way.
Release builds must do the same.
Do not change the key itself — CN=Closecode Debug is already installed on the user's phone and is what the installed app expects.
Related: build-tools/35.0.0 is installed on the phone but its shell wrappers start with #!/bin/bash, which Android does not have — 35.0.0/apksigner fails with "No such file or directory". 35.0.1 has been patched to #!/bin/sh and works. Either stop installing 35.0.0, or patch its wrappers the same way.
Done when
closecode build and ./gradlew assembleDebug produce APKs with an identical signer certificate SHA-256. The acceptance script checks exactly this, and it compares the real certificates, not the vault.

2. A JDK must be selectable, and the choice must reach every build path
The problem
Two JDKs can be installed, but nothing can choose between them. I installed JDK 21 with closecode setup --java21 — that works — and then found there is no selection at all.

The evidence
Route	Result
closecode config set jdk <path>	unknown key; use type, engine, sdk, buildTools, gradleVersion, nodeVersion, pythonVersion, env.<NAME> or ports.<name>
closecode toolchain	status only, read-only
closecode config set env.JAVA_HOME <path>	accepted, then ignored — the build succeeded with JAVA_HOME=/nonexistent-jdk-xyz
org.gradle.java.home in $GRADLE_USER_HOME/gradle.properties	obeyed by ./gradlew, ignored by closecode build — the build succeeded with …/NOT-A-REAL-JDK
After the install, the status does not even mention JDK 21:

$ closecode toolchain status --json
"jdk":{"path":"/data/user/0/com.closecode/file/g/lib/jvm/java-17-openjdk",
       "version":"openjdk version \"17.0.21\" 2026-10-20","ok":true}
And the two build paths can end up on different JDKs:

$ ./gradlew --version
Launcher JVM:  17.0.21 (Termux 17.0.21+0)
Daemon JVM:    /data/user/0/com.closecode/file/j/lib/jvm/java-21-openjdk (from org.gradle.java.home)
The daemon compiles on 21, the launcher runs on 17, and closecode build runs on 17. The same project can be compiled by three JDKs depending on the button.

Root cause — one line in the gradlew shim:

if [ -z "$JAVA_HOME" ] && [ -f "$PROPS" ]; then   # ← an inherited JAVA_HOME wins
Proof:

$ ./gradlew --version                  Launcher 17.0.21 | Daemon 21.0.13   ← mixed
$ env -u JAVA_HOME ./gradlew --version Launcher 21.0.13 | Daemon 21.0.13   ← correct
Why it matters
A user picks a JDK and the build uses a different one, or two paths use two different ones. AGP 8.x needs 17+; a project that needs a newer JDK silently builds with 17 and fails with a confusing message.

What to do
closecode toolchain status --json returns a jdks array with every installed JDK — path and version — plus which one is selected.
closecode config set jdk <path> is accepted, validated and persisted. (The test also accepts javaHome, java.home or jdkPath if you prefer one of those names.)
closecode build stops pinning its own JDK. Right now the build succeeds even when org.gradle.java.home points at a directory that does not exist, so it is overriding the setting. The launcher and the daemon must both run on the selected JDK.
The gradlew shim lets the selection beat an inherited JAVA_HOME. Remove the [ -z "$JAVA_HOME" ] guard so the selected JDK is always used.
closecode setup --java21 registers the JDK it installed, so it appears in toolchain status and in the Settings screen.
Done when
With 17 and 21 both installed, selecting 21 makes ./gradlew --version, closecode build and the editor's Gradle menu all report 21.0.13, and selecting 17 switches all three back.

3. python3 -m venv must work
The problem
$ python3 -m venv .venv
$ echo $?
1
Error: Command '[…/.venv/bin/python3', '-m', 'ensurepip', '--upgrade', '--default-pip']'
       returned non-zero exit status 1.
No .venv/bin/pip is created.

The exact cause
It is not missing wheels — it is a version mismatch inside the runtime:

$ python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade --default-pip
FileNotFoundError: [Errno 2] No such file or directory:
  '…/file/u/lib/python3.14/ensurepip/_bundled/pip-26.2.2-py3-none-any.whl'
ensurepip/__init__.py has _PIP_VERSION = "26.2.2", and because the runtime is built without WHEEL_PKG_DIR it falls back to ensurepip/_bundled/pip-{_PIP_VERSION}-py3-none-any.whl. The _bundled folder actually holds pip-25.2-py3-none-any.whl — the wheel from an older Python that was left behind when the runtime was upgraded to 3.14.10.

So the Python runtime and its own ensurepip bundle are out of sync.

Why it matters
Every Python project template (python-fastapi, python-cli) needs a working venv, and the failing part is invisible until a user tries to install a package.

What to do
Ship a runtime whose ensurepip bundle matches its own _PIP_VERSION. The wheel has to be pip-26.2.2-py3-none-any.whl for Python 3.14.10, or the runtime must be pinned to the Python version whose bundle it ships.
Make the upgrade path safe. When the runtime is updated, the _bundled directory must be updated with it — an old wheel surviving a runtime upgrade is exactly what happened here.
As a safety net, make venv creation fall back to uv venv / pip --python when ensurepip fails, instead of failing the whole venv.
Note
I put the correct wheel in place on the test phone by hand (ensurepip/_bundled/pip-26.2.2-py3-none-any.whl), so python3 -m venv works here today. That is a workaround, not the fix — it will be lost the next time the runtime is updated, which is exactly the failure this item is about. Please fix it in the app rather than relying on the phone.

Done when
python3 -m venv .venv && .venv/bin/pip --version exits 0 — after a runtime update, not only on a freshly provisioned phone.

4. Removing a component must not delete the dependency cache
The problem
GRADLE_USER_HOME is files/gradle/home, which sits inside the folder that "Remove Gradle tools" deletes. In build 77 that cost 1.6 GB and turned a 75-second build into 22 minutes.

Why it matters
A user frees a little space, and the next build downloads the whole dependency tree again — on a phone, on a slow connection.

What to do
Either move the cache out of the removed folder, or make removal leave home/ alone and give the cache its own "Clear dependency cache" action.

Done when
After removing Gradle, $GRADLE_USER_HOME still exists with its cache intact, and there is a separate action that clears it.

The acceptance script tests this one with --all, because the test destroys the cache:

sh closecode-acceptance.sh --all
5. A failed download must resume
The problem
In build 77, setup --gradle stopped at 37,597,769 of 136,983,045 bytes with {"error":"timeout"} and left no partial file behind, so the retry started from zero.

Why it matters
On a phone-sized connection, one flaky moment costs the user the whole download, and the "Install" button looks broken.

What to do
Keep the partial file and send a Range: header when retrying. A failed install must also leave the previous working version in place.

Done when
A download that is interrupted resumes from where it stopped, and a failed install leaves the previous working version installed.

Rules that apply to all five
One installer, two front ends. Anything in Settings must also be reachable from closecode setup, and the reverse.
The app installs, the user consents. The AI must never need curl, tar, chmod, or a hand-written license file.
Never destroy a working install or a cache to install or remove something.
One signing key for every engine and every build path. ← see item 1
A failed build exits non-zero with one root cause, diagnostics and a log path.
Reject unknown flags. closecode setup --native --help starts a 564 MB NDK download instead of printing help.
How to report back
When you are done, send exactly two things:

The output of the acceptance script — the whole board, including the PASS n FAIL n SKIP n line at the end.
One line per item — what you changed and in which file.
That is all. No report, no summary, no reproduction steps. The board is the report.

What gets checked when the user asks for a re-check
These are the exact things that will be re-measured on the phone:

#	Check	Command
1	closecode build and ./gradlew assembleDebug sign with the same certificate	apksigner verify --print-certs on both APKs
2	A JDK can be selected and every path follows it	closecode config set jdk <path>, closecode toolchain status --json, ./gradlew --version
3	python3 -m venv creates a working pip	python3 -m venv .venv
4	Removing Gradle leaves the cache	closecode setup --remove gradle, then du
5	A failed download resumes	interrupted download
Plus the regression checks: closecode build still exits 0 and produces a working APK, closecode check still exits 0, the toolchain is still complete, the Sizes in Settings still match the disk, and the Settings screens still show every component with a Remove button.

The test script
Save this as closecode-acceptance.sh next to the project and run:

sh closecode-acceptance.sh
It is written for Android's toybox, so it uses grep -E for alternation.

#!/system/bin/sh
# =============================================================================
# CloseCode acceptance test — run it ON THE PHONE, no computer needed.
#
#   sh closecode-acceptance.sh          safe checks only
#   sh closecode-acceptance.sh --all    also runs the destructive checks
#                                           (destroys the Gradle cache)
#
# Exit code 0 = everything passed. Non-zero = read the FAIL lines.
# Every check prints PASS / FAIL / SKIP with the number it measured.
#
# NOTE: this runs on Android's toybox, so it uses `grep -E` for alternation
# (toybox grep does not understand `\|`).
# =============================================================================

PASS=0
FAIL=0
SKIP=0
FAILED_LINES=""
ROOT=$(cd "$(dirname "$0")" && pwd)
TMP="$ROOT/.acceptance"
RUN_ALL="$1"

say()  { printf '%s\n' "$*"; }
ok()   { PASS=$((PASS+1)); printf '  PASS  %s\n' "$1"; }
bad()  { FAIL=$((FAIL+1)); FAILED_LINES="$FAILED_LINES
    - $1"; printf '  FAIL  %s\n' "$1"; }
skip() { SKIP=$((SKIP+1)); printf '  SKIP  %s\n' "$1"; }
head_() { printf '\n%s\n' "$1"; }

# ---- small helpers ----------------------------------------------------------
jget() {  # jget <file> <key>  -> first string value of key
  sed -n "s/.*\"$2\":\"\([^\"]*\)\".*/\1/p" "$1" 2>/dev/null | head -1
}
jnum() {  # jnum <file> <key>  -> first numeric value of key
  sed -n "s/.*\"$2\":\([0-9][0-9]*\).*/\1/p" "$1" 2>/dev/null | head -1
}
du_k() { du -sk "$1" 2>/dev/null | cut -f1; }

rm -rf "$TMP"; mkdir -p "$TMP"
STATUS="$TMP/status.json"

say "==========================================================="
say " CloseCode acceptance test"
say " project : $ROOT"
say " date    : $(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M')"
say "==========================================================="

# =============================================================================
head_ "1. Toolchain is installed and complete"
closecode toolchain status --json > "$STATUS" 2>/dev/null
if [ ! -s "$STATUS" ]; then
  bad "closecode toolchain status produced no output"
else
  GRADLE_HOME=$(jget "$STATUS" gradleUserHome)
  SDK_ROOT=$(jget "$STATUS" sdkRoot)
  JDK_PATH=$(sed -n 's/.*"jdk":{[^}]*"path":"\([^"]*\)".*/\1/p' "$STATUS" | head -1)

  [ -n "$JDK_PATH" ] && [ -x "$JDK_PATH/bin/java" ] \
    && ok "JDK installed: $JDK_PATH" || bad "no usable JDK reported by toolchain status"

  [ -n "$GRADLE_HOME" ] && [ -d "$GRADLE_HOME" ] \
    && ok "GRADLE_USER_HOME exists: $GRADLE_HOME" || bad "GRADLE_USER_HOME missing"

  [ -n "$SDK_ROOT" ] && [ -d "$SDK_ROOT" ] \
    && ok "SDK root exists: $SDK_ROOT" || bad "SDK root missing"

  grep -q '"api":35,"path":"[^"]*","ok":true' "$STATUS" \
    && ok "Android platform 35 installed" || bad "Android platform 35 not installed"

  grep -q '"buildTools":\[{"version"' "$STATUS" \
    && ok "build-tools installed" || bad "build-tools not installed"

  [ -d "$SDK_ROOT/platform-tools" ] \
    && ok "platform-tools installed" || bad "platform-tools missing"

  if [ -f "$SDK_ROOT/licenses/android-sdk-license" ]; then
    ok "SDK licenses accepted ($(ls "$SDK_ROOT/licenses" | wc -l | tr -d ' ') files)"
  else
    bad "SDK licenses missing (android-sdk-license not found)"
  fi

  [ -d "$SDK_ROOT/ndk" ] && [ -d "$SDK_ROOT/cmake" ] \
    && ok "NDK and CMake installed" || bad "NDK or CMake missing"
fi

# =============================================================================
head_ "2. The size the app shows matches the disk"
if [ -n "$SDK_ROOT" ] && [ -d "$SDK_ROOT" ]; then
  REPORTED=$(jnum "$STATUS" sdkBytes)
  # du of everything in the SDK except the native toolchain, which the app
  # reports separately under "Native tools".
  ONDISK=0
  for D in build-tools platforms platform-tools licenses; do
    K=$(du_k "$SDK_ROOT/$D" 2>/dev/null)
    [ -n "$K" ] && ONDISK=$((ONDISK + K))
  done
  REPORTED_K=0
  [ -n "$REPORTED" ] && REPORTED_K=$((REPORTED / 1024))
  if [ "$ONDISK" -gt 0 ] && [ "$REPORTED_K" -gt 0 ]; then
    say "        app reports ${REPORTED_K} KB, disk has ${ONDISK} KB"
    # A previous build showed 3.6 GB for 1.8 GB of NDK + CMake, exactly double.
    # Catch that class of bug without demanding byte-exact accounting.
    if [ "$REPORTED_K" -gt $((ONDISK * 3 / 2)) ] || [ "$REPORTED_K" -lt $((ONDISK / 2)) ]; then
      bad "reported SDK size is more than 1.5x away from the disk"
    else
      ok "reported SDK size is in the right range"
    fi
  else
    skip "no size reported by toolchain status"
  fi
else
  skip "SDK root missing"
fi

# =============================================================================
head_ "3. closecode build produces a signed APK"
# Remove the old logs so check 9 only sees what this build writes.
rm -f "$ROOT/build/logs/build.log" "$ROOT/build/logs/exec.log" 2>/dev/null
closecode build > "$TMP/build.json" 2>&1
BUILD_EXIT=$?
APK="$ROOT/app/build/outputs/apk/debug/app-debug.apk"
if [ "$BUILD_EXIT" -eq 0 ] && [ -f "$APK" ]; then
  ok "closecode build exited 0 ($(wc -c < "$APK" | tr -d ' ') bytes)"
  cp "$APK" "$TMP/apk-pipeline.apk"
else
  bad "closecode build exited $BUILD_EXIT"
  tail -c 400 "$TMP/build.json" | sed 's/^/        /'
fi

# =============================================================================
head_ "4. ./gradlew assembleDebug succeeds"
if [ -x "$ROOT/gradlew" ]; then
  ( cd "$ROOT" && ./gradlew assembleDebug ) > "$TMP/gradlew.log" 2>&1
  GW_EXIT=$?
  if [ "$GW_EXIT" -eq 0 ]; then
    ok "./gradlew assembleDebug exited 0"
    cp "$ROOT/app/build/outputs/apk/debug/app-debug.apk" "$TMP/apk-gradle.apk"
  else
    bad "./gradlew assembleDebug exited $GW_EXIT"
    grep -iE "error|what went wrong" "$TMP/gradlew.log" | head -3 | sed 's/^/        /'
  fi
else
  skip "no gradlew shim in the project"
fi

# =============================================================================
head_ "5. Both build paths sign with the same key"
[ -n "$JAVA_HOME" ] || export JAVA_HOME="$JDK_PATH"
# Some installed build-tools have '#!/bin/bash' shebangs, which Android does not
# have, so try every apksigner until one actually answers.
cert_of() {  # cert_of <apk> -> signer certificate SHA-256, or empty
  for C in "$SDK_ROOT"/build-tools/*/apksigner; do
    [ -f "$C" ] || continue
    OUT=$("$C" verify --print-certs "$1" 2>/dev/null | grep "SHA-256 digest" | head -1 | sed 's/.*digest: //')
    if [ -n "$OUT" ]; then echo "$OUT"; return; fi
  done
  echo ""
}
if [ ! -f "$TMP/apk-pipeline.apk" ] || [ ! -f "$TMP/apk-gradle.apk" ]; then
  skip "no APK from one of the two paths"
else
  CERT_A=$(cert_of "$TMP/apk-pipeline.apk")
  CERT_B=$(cert_of "$TMP/apk-gradle.apk")
  say "        closecode build : ${CERT_A:-none}"
  say "        ./gradlew       : ${CERT_B:-none}"
  if [ -z "$CERT_A" ] || [ -z "$CERT_B" ]; then
    skip "no working apksigner found (build-tools scripts may need /bin/sh)"
  elif [ "$CERT_A" = "$CERT_B" ]; then
    ok "both build paths sign with the same key"
  else
    bad "the two build paths use different signing keys"
  fi
  if [ "$CERT_A" = "30fae35d64645323304340c4f570a58377de8153477a5e334dc62c036a87f979" ]; then
    ok "the key is CN=Closecode Debug"
  else
    bad "the key is not CN=Closecode Debug"
  fi
fi

# =============================================================================
head_ "6. A failed build exits non-zero with one clear cause"
GRADLE_FILE="$ROOT/app/build.gradle.kts"
if [ -f "$GRADLE_FILE" ]; then
  cp "$GRADLE_FILE" "$TMP/build.gradle.kts.bak"
  printf '\nthis is not valid kotlin !!!\n' >> "$GRADLE_FILE"
  ( cd "$ROOT" && closecode build ) > "$TMP/badbuild.log" 2>&1
  BAD_EXIT=$?
  cp "$TMP/build.gradle.kts.bak" "$GRADLE_FILE"        # always put it back
  if [ "$BAD_EXIT" -ne 0 ]; then
    ok "a broken project exits $BAD_EXIT (non-zero)"
    if grep -qE "Gradle failed \(exit 1\)" "$TMP/badbuild.log"; then
      ok "the failure names one root cause"
      grep -o '"summary":"[^"]*"' "$TMP/badbuild.log" | head -1 | sed 's/^/        /'
    else
      bad "the failure does not name one root cause"
    fi
    if grep -qE "[a-zA-Z]+\.log" "$TMP/badbuild.log"; then
      ok "the failure output names a log file"
    else
      bad "the failure output does not name a log file"
    fi
  else
    bad "a broken project built successfully"
  fi
else
  skip "no app/build.gradle.kts to break"
fi

# =============================================================================
head_ "7. A JDK can be chosen, and the choice is real"
JDK21="/data/user/0/com.closecode/file/j/lib/jvm/java-21-openjdk"
if [ ! -x "$JDK21/bin/java" ]; then
  skip "JDK 21 is not installed (closecode setup --java21)"
else
  # a) some command must accept a JDK selection
  ACCEPTED=""
  for KEY in jdk javaHome java.home jdkPath java; do
    OUT=$(closecode config set "$KEY" "$JDK21" 2>&1)
    if ! echo "$OUT" | grep -qi "unknown key"; then
      ACCEPTED="$KEY"
      break
    fi
  done
  if [ -n "$ACCEPTED" ]; then
    ok "closecode config set $ACCEPTED <jdk> is accepted"
  else
    bad "no command can select a JDK (config set rejects every key)"
  fi

  # b) the toolchain status must show the selection
  closecode toolchain status --json > "$STATUS" 2>/dev/null
  if grep -q "java-21-openjdk" "$STATUS"; then
    ok "toolchain status reports the selected JDK 21"
  else
    bad "toolchain status does not report the selected JDK 21"
  fi

  # c) the wrapper must use it for launcher AND daemon
  ( cd "$ROOT" && env -u JAVA_HOME ./gradlew --version ) > "$TMP/version.log" 2>&1
  LAUNCH=$(grep "Launcher JVM" "$TMP/version.log" | grep -oE '[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+' | head -1)
  DAEMON=$(grep "Daemon JVM" "$TMP/version.log" | grep -oE 'java-[0-9]+' | head -1)
  if [ "$DAEMON" = "java-21" ]; then
    ok "./gradlew daemon runs on the selected JDK ($DAEMON)"
  else
    bad "./gradlew daemon runs on ${DAEMON:-unknown}, expected java-21"
  fi
  case "$LAUNCH" in
    21.*) ok "./gradlew launcher runs on the selected JDK ($LAUNCH)" ;;
    *)    bad "./gradlew launcher runs on ${LAUNCH:-unknown}, expected 21.x" ;;
  esac

  # d) closecode build must use the same JDK
  closecode build > "$TMP/build21.json" 2>&1
  if grep -qE "21\.0\." "$ROOT/build/logs/exec.log" 2>/dev/null \
     || grep -qE "21\.0\." "$ROOT/build/logs/build.log" 2>/dev/null; then
    ok "closecode build ran on JDK 21"
  else
    bad "closecode build did not run on JDK 21"
    head -2 "$ROOT/build/logs/exec.log" 2>/dev/null | sed 's/^/        /'
  fi

  # put it back
  if [ -n "$ACCEPTED" ]; then closecode config unset "$ACCEPTED" >/dev/null 2>&1; fi
fi

# =============================================================================
head_ "8. python3 -m venv works"
( cd "$TMP" && python3 -m venv venvtest ) > "$TMP/venv.log" 2>&1
if [ -x "$TMP/venvtest/bin/pip" ] && "$TMP/venvtest/bin/pip" --version >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  ok "python3 -m venv creates a working pip"
else
  bad "python3 -m venv does not create a working pip"
  tail -2 "$TMP/venv.log" | sed 's/^/        /'
fi

# =============================================================================
head_ "9. The build log contains no error"
if [ -f "$ROOT/build/logs/build.log" ]; then
  if grep -qE "^e: |^FAILURE:|BUILD FAILED" "$ROOT/build/logs/build.log"; then
    bad "build/logs/build.log contains a build error"
    grep -m2 -E "^e: |^FAILURE:|BUILD FAILED" "$ROOT/build/logs/build.log" | sed 's/^/        /'
  else
    ok "build/logs/build.log is clean"
  fi
else
  skip "no build/logs/build.log"
fi
if [ -f "$ROOT/build/logs/exec.log" ]; then
  if grep -qE "Error|Exception" "$ROOT/build/logs/exec.log"; then
    bad "build/logs/exec.log contains an error"
    grep -m2 -E "Error|Exception" "$ROOT/build/logs/exec.log" | sed 's/^/        /'
  else
    ok "build/logs/exec.log is clean"
  fi
else
  skip "the build does not write build/logs/exec.log"
fi

# =============================================================================
head_ "10. Removing a component keeps the dependency cache"
if [ "$RUN_ALL" = "--all" ]; then
  say "        removing Gradle and reinstalling — this takes about 25 minutes"
  GRADLE_HOME=$(jget "$STATUS" gradleUserHome)
  before=$(du_k "$GRADLE_HOME" 2>/dev/null)
  closecode setup --remove gradle > "$TMP/remove.log" 2>&1
  if [ -d "$GRADLE_HOME" ]; then
    after=$(du_k "$GRADLE_HOME" 2>/dev/null)
    say "        cache before ${before} KB, after ${after} KB"
    if [ "$after" -ge "$before" ]; then
      ok "removing Gradle kept the dependency cache"
    else
      bad "removing Gradle deleted the dependency cache"
    fi
    closecode setup --gradle > "$TMP/reinstall.log" 2>&1
    ok "Gradle reinstalled"
  else
    bad "removing Gradle deleted $GRADLE_HOME (the whole cache)"
    closecode setup --gradle > "$TMP/reinstall.log" 2>&1
    ok "Gradle reinstalled"
  fi
else
  skip "destructive — run with --all to test it"
fi

# =============================================================================
head_ "11. A failed download resumes"
skip "needs a network that fails on purpose — test manually"

# =============================================================================
say ""
say "==========================================================="
say " PASS $PASS   FAIL $FAIL   SKIP $SKIP"
if [ "$FAIL" -gt 0 ]; then
  say ""
  say " Still failing:"
  printf '%s\n' "$FAILED_LINES"
fi
say "==========================================================="
rm -rf "$TMP"
[ "$FAIL" -eq 0 ] && exit 0
exit 1